# Changing the Box Model Calculation

By default, `box-sizing: content-box;` means width/height = content only. This often leads to unexpected total sizes.

`box-sizing: border-box;` makes width/height include content, padding, and border. The content area shrinks to accommodate padding and border, keeping the outer size constant.

```css
.box {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 200px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 5px solid;
  /* actual width stays 200px, content becomes 150px */
}
```
This is much more intuitive for layout.

# Set It and Forget It

Almost all modern CSS projects start with this rule:

```css
*, *::before, *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
```
This makes every element (and its pseudo‑elements) use `border-box`, so declaring a width of `50%` actually gives you a box that is 50% of its parent, padding and borders included.

It eliminates the mental math of `content-box` and prevents layout bugs. This reset is in every popular CSS framework.
